
  In a single-browser prototype, [3msomeone else changing a task[0m has to be faked somehow. And here's the catch: [1mtoasts only
[0m  [1mmake sense if a change can arrive while you're sitting there.[0m If the only way to generate activity is to switch to
  Bob, edit a task, and switch back to Alice, then by the time you're Alice the moment has passed — the notification
  lands in the bell, and the toast never fires for the person meant to see it. So your toast choice quietly requires an
  asynchronous actor.

  - [1mUser switcher only[0m — an "acting as: Alice / Bob / Carol" control. Deterministic and precise: you can demonstrate an
    exact rule ("Alice watches this, Bob changes it, Alice gets told"). But as above, it makes toasts nearly
    unreachable.
  - [1mSimulated teammates[0m — a background timer where fake users edit tasks every few seconds. The feed fills on its own
    and toasts fire naturally. Costs: non-deterministic, so you can't reliably demo a [3mspecific[0m rule, and random activity
    can look like noise rather than design.
  - [1mBoth[0m — switcher as the primary mechanism, plus a "simulate team activity" toggle you can turn on. Deliberate demos
    when you want to prove a rule, ambient activity when you want to show the feature breathing.
  - [1mSeed data only[0m — preload a notification history, generate nothing live. Cheapest by far, but nothing ever arrives,
    so toasts become dead code and the bell is a static list.

  [1mMy recommendation: both, with the simulator behind an off-by-default toggle.[0m The switcher is what proves the
  subscription logic is real; the simulator is what makes toasts worth having. The toggle keeps random activity from
  interfering when you're trying to demonstrate something specific.
